  <page>
    <definition xml:id="def-tree">
      <statement>
        <p workspace="1cm">
          A vertex <m>v</m> in a graph <m>\GVE</m> is called a <term>leaf</term> provided that
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <theorem xml:id="thm-tree-leaves">
      <statement>
        <p workspace="5cm">
          Every tree on at least two vertices has at least two leaves.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </theorem>
    <theorem xml:id="thm-tree-unique-path">
      <statement>
        <p workspace="7cm">
          If <m>\bfT</m> is a tree, then for every pair of distinct vertices <m>u,v</m>, there exists a unique path from <m>u</m> to <m>v</m> in <m>\bfT</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </theorem>
  </page>
  <page>
    <activity>
      <statement>
        <p workspace="6cm">
          <ol marker="a.">
            <li>With your neighbors, use mathematical induction to prove that every tree on <m>n</m> vertices has exactly <m>n-1</m> edges.
            </li>
            <li>
              How many edges would an <m>n</m>-vertex forest consisting of <m>k</m> trees have?
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </activity>
  </page>
  </handout>
  <handout xml:id="notes-eulerham">
    <title>Eulerian and Hamiltonian Graphs</title>
    <p>Let <m>\bfG</m> be a graph without isolated vertices. We say that <m>\bfG</m> is <term>eulerian</term><idx>
      <h>graph</h>
      <h>eulerian</h>
    </idx> provided that there is a sequence <m>(x_0,x_1,x_2,\dots,x_t)</m> of
    vertices from <m>\bfG</m>, with repetition allowed, so that <ol>
      <li><m>x_0=x_t</m>;</li>
      <li>for every <m>i=0,1,\dots, t-1</m>, <m>x_ix_{i+1}</m> is an edge of <m>\bfG</m>;</li>
      <li>for every edge <m>e\in E</m>, there is a unique integer <m>i</m> with <m>0\le i\lt t</m>
    for which <m>e=x_ix_{i+1}</m>.</li>
    </ol>
  </p>
  <p>When <m>\bfG</m> is eulerian, a sequence satisfying these three conditions is called an <term>eulerian
    circuit</term><idx>
      <h>eulerian</h>
      <h>circuit</h>
    </idx>. A sequence of vertices <m>(x_0,x_1,\dots,x_t)</m> is called a <term>
    circuit</term><idx>
      <h>circuit</h>
    </idx> when it satisfies only the first two of these conditions.
  </p>
  <algorithm xml:id="alg-eulerian">
    <title>Eulerian Circuit Finder</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        <dl>
          <li>
            <title>Input</title>
            <p>
              A graph <m>\bfG=([n],E)</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>Output</title>
            <p>
              An eulerian circuit <m>C</m> in <m>\bfG</m>, a vertex of odd degree in <m>\bfG</m>, or a connected component of <m>\bfG</m> and an edge of <m>\bfG</m> that is not in that connected component.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>
      <p>
        <ol marker="1.">
          <li>
            <p>
              Initialize <m>C=(1)</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li xml:id="step-eulerian-subcircuit">
            <p>
              While not every edge of <m>\bfG</m> is traversed, determine if any vertex of <m>C</m> is incident with an edge that has not been traversed. 
              <ol marker="a.">
                <li>
                  <p>
                    If all vertices of <m>C</m> have all their edges traversed, then return the vertices of <m>C</m> as a connected component of <m>\bfG</m> with an edge not traversed by <m>C</m> demonstrating that <m>\bfG</m> is not connected.
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li >
                  <p>
                    If <m>C</m> has a vertex incident with an edge that has not been traversed, call that vertex <m>u_0</m>. Construct a walk <m>W</m> starting from <m>u_0</m>. From vertex <m>u_i</m>, follow the edge traversed by neither <m>C</m> nor <m>W</m> going to the neighbor of <m>u_i</m> with smallest label.
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    The construction of <m>W</m> halts at a vertex <m>u_s</m> for which all edges have been traversed.
                    <ol marker="i.">
                      <li>
                        <p>
                          If <m>u_s\neq u_0</m>, then return <m>u_0</m> as a vertex of odd degree, showing that <m>\bfG</m> is not eulerian.
                        </p>
                      </li>
                      <li>
                        <p>
                          If <m>u_s = u_0</m>, update the circuit <m>C</m> by replacing <m>u_0</m> in <m>C</m> with the walk <m>W</m>. Continue iterating by returning to <xref ref="step-eulerian-subcircuit">step</xref>.
                        </p>
                      </li>
                    </ol>
                  </p>
                </li>

              </ol> 
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Return <m>C</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </algorithm>
